 for ways that  manufacturers can embed  identification on the devices because  right now these  units aren't marked in any way so if  it's marked  and traceable back it'll hopefully  diminish their ability to get rid of  some of this product  police advise where possible park  vehicles inside or in well-lit  areas or where there are cameras because  with prices as high as they are for  precious metals  even a few grams from several converters  can be a lucrative night's work for  thieves  eric sorensen global news toronto  caring for health care workers still  ahead the burnout behind canada's  pandemic front line  as we approach what appears to be a  semblance of normal life there will be a  need to take stock of what's been lost  in the last year  including the toll on those who care for  covet 19 patients  the physical and psychological strain  has left some deep wounds  and will take time to heal tonight karen  lieberman  has some of their stories  when it became clear there was a need  for help on the front lines  kelly cray answered the call we're right  there  at the greatest risks as a respiratory  therapist cray is a crucial part of the  fight against covid19  helping patients to breathe and often  providing comfort as they take their  final breaths  we see them at their worst and we hope  we get them through  through it with as minimal  amount of casualties as possible she  says it's been an emotional roller  coaster  certainly she's not alone for the ride  the pandemic has presented an  unprecedented challenge  not just for society but for those  providing the public health response  as health care workers just all very  burnt out from the mental burden the  emotional burden  being on the front line caring for  patients with covid19  and navigating a year-long global health  crisis  adds even more pressure to an already  high stress job  emergency physician shasma mathani has  had to balance the needs of her  patients with the demands of her young  family  recently she gave up a shift because she  needed a mental health break  i didn't have the ability or the  resiliency to bounce back the way that i  had before the pandemic dr jillian  horton just published a book about her  own struggle with burnout  we are all perfectly fine ask the  question  when we need help we count on doctors  but what happens  when doctors fall apart physicians are  really  bad for this in terms of how we talk to  one another how we inquire about how our  colleagues are  are doing we we don't have a culture  where we talk a lot about the times when  we're not doing  fine that can be especially problematic  because burnout  may be easily hidden behind the mask so  we're burying a huge burden of caring  and i think it's compounded by  the fact that we're working longer we're  working more dr julianne irving  specializes in physician wellness and is  getting referrals from doctors  needing support and feeling exhausted  people might not have full-blown ptsd  but are certainly having some of the  symptoms  so not sleeping as well feeling  chronically anxious having difficulty  turning their fight or flight system off  if anyone understands trauma it's kelly  cray  what the public sees through the camera  is not necessarily what goes on here is  is  tiring frustrating  and that takes a toll karen lieberman  global news toronto  karen looks at how health care workers  are finding creative ways to cope  with burnout  staying hopeful at home next the  canadian who just keeps dancing  you're watching global national  [Music]  politicians sometimes dodge reporters  questions but  thailand's prime minister took it to a  new level during a news conference he  was asked about a possible cabinet  shuffle  he not only refused to answer the he  then sprayed the front row of reporters  with sanitizer the quick-tempered former  army commander who overthrew the elected  government in thailand in 2014  is known for his impulsive behavior  well lots of people are short-tempered  and tired these days and it's no wonder  after a year of living through the  pandemic  of course it is wise to look for the  positives and some of us have embraced  spending time at home but  let's face it puzzles baking and binge  watching can only keep you interested  for so long  mike trolley examines the ups and downs  of the last year  remember the shock of that first  lockdown we went from  bustling cities to eerie quiet overnight  but we adapted even embraced the  isolation by spending more time doing  family activities  we took up hobbies like baking so many  goodies big and small  and the fails they were even better but  as the weather warmed up the lustre of  the virtual  began to fade i just can't be on another  zoom call  i almost refuse i'm like i'm over it i'm  over zoom  and that was the beginning of the slump  i'm not a cat by winter it was on full  display as participation in virtual or  distance events  bottomed out definitely totally not  doing the zoom happy hours anymore  or drive by birthdays you can't see it  in one bit and  in a in a bachelor watching tv all the  time  you have a cigarette then you end up  smoking all cigarettes and it's not good  for you  but the slump was more than frustration  and annoyance  antidepressant prescriptions went up 20  according to some insurance companies  and even the most upbeat people admitted  to feeling down  yeah i i have my moments of reflections  too  during my day time and when that happens  dradeep pander  dances over the last year  millions of people have watched gurdeep  do traditional bangla dances  in the wilderness near his yukon cottage  and he's become  so popular he no longer does it work for  the government  like so many letters from all over  the world right now these are not just  letters i received yesterday  if he ever has a day when he feels a  little down he recalls messages like the  one he received from a girl immobilized  in a hospital bed  and i watch your videos and i dance  i do little dancing in the bed  it was such a powerful message i watched  the video i could oh my god it's  it's a really huge yeah it's huge  and hopefully all of this along with the  slump is over soon  mike drolite global news toronto and  that is global national for this  wednesday i'm donna friesen  tonight's your canada is hopetown on the  gaspe peninsula in quebec  please email us a picture of your canada  to viewers at globalnational.com  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow  take care of yourselves bye
 

